To integrate Cloudflare Tunnel creation and token management directly into Wintersys ADT, you can add a two-part lifecycle hook using the Cloudflare Tunnel API: a build-machine provisioning script to create the tunnel and route DNS, and a webserver bootstrap snippet to install cloudflared using the retrieved token. [1] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/networks/connectors/cloudflare-tunnel/get-started/create-remote-tunnel-api/)
Because Cloudflare supports remotely managed tunnels ("config_src": "cloudflare"), your build machine can create a single tunnel per deployment, configure its public hostname ingress rules via API, store the resulting CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN, and pass that same token to every webserver (including autoscaled replicas). [1] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/configuration/), [2] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/networks/connectors/cloudflare-tunnel/get-started/create-remote-tunnel-api/)

1. Prerequisites: Cloudflare API Token Permissions

Ensure your CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN in your ADT configuration includes these permissions: [1] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/networks/connectors/cloudflare-tunnel/get-started/create-remote-tunnel-api/)
• Account -> Cloudflare Tunnel -> Edit (or Cloudflare One Connectors Write)
• Zone -> DNS -> Edit (to create the CNAME record pointing to <tunnel_id>.cfargotunnel.com) [1] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/networks/connectors/cloudflare-tunnel/get-started/create-remote-tunnel-api/), [2] (https://blog.rullo.fr/use-cloudflare-tunnels-as-an-ingress-for-k8s-df21cefc4403)

2. Build Machine Script: ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh

Place this script in your build machine's DNS/network services directory (e.g., ${BUILD_HOME}/services/dns/cloudflare/ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh). It follows your exact Wintersys ADT conventions (#!/bin/sh, #: help header, LogStatusMessages.sh, and jq): [1]
sh
#!/bin/sh
#: Name:        ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh
#: Purpose:     Creates a remotely-managed Cloudflare Tunnel, configures ingress, binds DNS, and outputs the connector token.
#: Author:      Peter Winter
#: Date:        26/11/2026
#: Version:     1.0
#:
#: Usage:       ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh <account_id> <zone_id> <api_token> <tunnel_name> <public_hostname> <origin_url>
#:              ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh [-h|--help]
#:
#: Parameters:
#:   $1         [Required] Cloudflare Account ID.
#:   $2         [Required] Cloudflare Zone ID for the target domain.
#:   $3         [Required] Cloudflare API Bearer Token with Tunnel:Edit and DNS:Edit permissions.
#:   $4         [Required] Unique label for the Cloudflare Tunnel (e.g., adt-prod-tunnel).
#:   $5         [Required] Public FQDN to route through the tunnel (e.g., www.example.com).
#:   $6         [Required] Local origin service URL on the webserver (e.g., http://localhost:80).
#:
#: Environment:
#:   BUILD_HOME Resolved automatically from /home/buildhome.dat
#:
#: Dependencies:
#:   - /home/buildhome.dat
#:   - LogStatusMessages.sh
#:   - External binaries: cat, echo, grep, cut, curl, jq
#:
#: Exit Codes:
#:   0          Tunnel provisioned, DNS bound, and token saved to ${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat
#:   1          Missing arguments or Cloudflare API failure
###################################################################################
# License Agreement:
# This file is part of The Wintersys Agile Deployment Toolkit.
# The Agile Deployment Toolkit is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
# it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
# the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
# (at your option) any later version.
# The Agile Deployment Toolkit is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
# but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
# MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
# GNU General Public License for more details.
# You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
# along with The Agile Deployment Toolkit.  If not, see <http://gnu.org>.
####################################################################################

#set -x
set -e

if [ "${1}" = "-h" ] || [ "${1}" = "--help" ]
then
        /usr/bin/grep '^#:' "$0" | /usr/bin/cut -c 4-
        exit 0
fi

if [ -r /home/buildhome.dat ]
then
        BUILD_HOME="$(/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat)"
else
        /bin/echo "Application framework configuration file missing at /home/buildhome.dat" >&2
        exit 1
fi

status()
{
        "${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/services/LogStatusMessages.sh" "${1}"
}

if [ -z "${1}" ] || [ -z "${2}" ] || [ -z "${3}" ] || [ -z "${4}" ] || [ -z "${5}" ] || [ -z "${6}" ]
then
        status "Error: 6 arguments are required"
        status "Usage: $0 <account_id> <zone_id> <api_token> <tunnel_name> <public_hostname> <origin_url>"
        exit 1
fi

account_id="${1}"
zone_id="${2}"
api_token="${3}"
tunnel_name="${4}"
public_hostname="${5}"
origin_url="${6}"

cf_api_base="https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4"
token_store="${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat"
id_store="${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_id.dat"

# 1. Check if a tunnel with this name already exists (idempotency for rebuilds)
set +e
existing_resp="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X GET "${cf_api_base}/accounts/${account_id}/cfd_tunnel?name=${tunnel_name}&is_deleted=false" \
        -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
        -H "Content-Type: application/json")"
curl_status="$?"
set -e

if [ "${curl_status}" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "${existing_resp}" ]
then
        status "Error: Failed to query existing Cloudflare Tunnels."
        exit 1
fi

tunnel_id="$(/bin/echo "${existing_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.result[0].id // empty')"
tunnel_token=""

# 2. Create the remotely-managed tunnel if it does not exist
if [ -z "${tunnel_id}" ]
then
        status "Creating Cloudflare Tunnel '${tunnel_name}'..."
        create_payload="$(/usr/bin/jq -n --arg name "${tunnel_name}" '{name: $name, config_src: "cloudflare"}')"

        set +e
        create_resp="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X POST "${cf_api_base}/accounts/${account_id}/cfd_tunnel" \
                -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
                -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
                --data "${create_payload}")"
        curl_status="$?"
        set -e

        success_flag="$(/bin/echo "${create_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.success // false')"
        if [ "${curl_status}" -ne 0 ] || [ "${success_flag}" != "true" ]
        then
                status "Error: Failed to create Cloudflare Tunnel '${tunnel_name}': ${create_resp}"
                exit 1
        fi

        tunnel_id="$(/bin/echo "${create_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.result.id // empty')"
        tunnel_token="$(/bin/echo "${create_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.result.token // empty')"
else
        status "Reusing existing Cloudflare Tunnel '${tunnel_name}' (${tunnel_id})..."
fi

# 3. Fetch the connector token explicitly if not returned during creation
if [ -z "${tunnel_token}" ]
then
        set +e
        token_resp="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X GET "${cf_api_base}/accounts/${account_id}/cfd_tunnel/${tunnel_id}/token" \
                -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
                -H "Content-Type: application/json")"
        curl_status="$?"
        set -e

        tunnel_token="$(/bin/echo "${token_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.result // empty')"
fi

if [ -z "${tunnel_id}" ] || [ -z "${tunnel_token}" ]
then
        status "Error: Could not resolve Cloudflare Tunnel ID or connector token."
        exit 1
fi

# 4. Push ingress routing rules (public_hostname -> origin_url + required 404 catch-all)
status "Configuring Cloudflare Tunnel ingress for '${public_hostname}' -> '${origin_url}'..."
ingress_payload="$(/usr/bin/jq -n \
        --arg host "${public_hostname}" \
        --arg svc "${origin_url}" \
        '{config: {ingress: [{hostname: $host, service: $svc, originRequest: {}}, {service: "http_status:404"}]}}')"

set +e
config_resp="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X PUT "${cf_api_base}/accounts/${account_id}/cfd_tunnel/${tunnel_id}/configurations" \
        -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
        -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
        --data "${ingress_payload}")"
curl_status="$?"
set -e

config_ok="$(/bin/echo "${config_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.success // false')"
if [ "${curl_status}" -ne 0 ] || [ "${config_ok}" != "true" ]
then
        status "Error: Failed to update tunnel ingress rules: ${config_resp}"
        exit 1
fi

# 5. Create or update the proxied CNAME DNS record pointing to <tunnel_id>.cfargotunnel.com
tunnel_cname="${tunnel_id}.cfargotunnel.com"
set +e
dns_lookup="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X GET "${cf_api_base}/zones/${zone_id}/dns_records?type=CNAME&name=${public_hostname}" \
        -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
        -H "Content-Type: application/json")"
set -e

dns_record_id="$(/bin/echo "${dns_lookup}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.result[0].id // empty')"
dns_payload="$(/usr/bin/jq -n \
        --arg name "${public_hostname}" \
        --arg content "${tunnel_cname}" \
        '{type: "CNAME", name: $name, content: $content, proxied: true}')"

if [ -z "${dns_record_id}" ]
then
        set +e
        dns_resp="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X POST "${cf_api_base}/zones/${zone_id}/dns_records" \
                -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
                -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
                --data "${dns_payload}")"
        set -e
else
        set +e
        dns_resp="$(/usr/bin/curl -sS -X PUT "${cf_api_base}/zones/${zone_id}/dns_records/${dns_record_id}" \
                -H "Authorization: Bearer ${api_token}" \
                -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
                --data "${dns_payload}")"
        set -e
fi

dns_ok="$(/bin/echo "${dns_resp}" | /usr/bin/jq -r '.success // false')"
if [ "${dns_ok}" != "true" ]
then
        status "Error: Failed to bind Cloudflare DNS CNAME for '${public_hostname}': ${dns_resp}"
        exit 1
fi

# 6. Persist Tunnel ID and Token securely in runtimedata for webserver/autoscaler provisioning
/usr/bin/mkdir -p "${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata"
/bin/echo "${tunnel_id}" > "${id_store}"
/bin/echo "${tunnel_token}" > "${token_store}"
/bin/chmod 600 "${id_store}" "${token_store}"

status "Success: Cloudflare Tunnel '${tunnel_name}' (${tunnel_id}) active for '${public_hostname}'."
exit 0
Use code with caution.

3. Webserver / Autoscaler Side: Attaching a Replica

Once ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh saves the token to ${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat, your build machine passes that token to every webserver (whether on Multipass or Linode) during provisioning or scaling.
Inside your webserver setup script (e.g., InstallCloudflared.sh), run:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e

tunnel_token="${1}"

if [ -z "${tunnel_token}" ]
then
        /bin/echo "No Cloudflare Tunnel token supplied; skipping cloudflared setup."
        exit 0
fi

if ! command -v cloudflared >/dev/null 2>&1
then
        /usr/bin/mkdir -p /usr/share/keyrings
        /usr/bin/curl -fsSL https://cloudflare.com | /usr/bin/tee /usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg >/dev/null
        codename="$(. /etc/os-release && /bin/echo "${VERSION_CODENAME}")"
        /bin/echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg] https://cloudflare.com ${codename} main" | /usr/bin/tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/cloudflared.list >/dev/null
        /usr/bin/apt-get update -qq
        /usr/bin/apt-get install -y -qq cloudflared
fi

# Register and start the systemd service (automatically load-balances across all active replicas)
/usr/bin/cloudflared service install "${tunnel_token}"
Use code with caution.

How This Fits Your Autoscaling & Multipass Workflow

• Idempotent Rebuilds: If you tear down your Multipass VMs and rebuild, ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh detects the existing tunnel by name, retrieves its token via GET /accounts/{account_id}/cfd_tunnel/{tunnel_id}/token, updates the ingress/DNS, and hands the token to your fresh VMs. [1] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/reference/tunnel-tokens/)
• Zero-API Autoscaling: When your autoscaler launches a second or third webserver (either locally via Multipass or on Linode), it reads ${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat and runs cloudflared service install. Cloudflare immediately adds the new VM as a load-balanced replica without any DNS API calls. [1] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/configuration/), [2] (https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/reference/tunnel-tokens/)
