You add a webserver to the tunnel simply by installing cloudflared on that webserver and running cloudflared service install <TUNNEL_TOKEN>—no API calls or dashboard changes needed.
Because ProvisionCloudflareTunnel.sh already configured the tunnel's routing rules on Cloudflare's side (public_hostname → http://localhost:80), any webserver that starts cloudflared using that exact same token automatically registers itself as an active origin for your website.
Here are the three ways to do this in your Wintersys ADT workflow, depending on how you are provisioning the webserver:

Method 1: Automatically at Boot via cloud-init (user_data_path)

This is the cleanest way for both Multipass and Linode, because the webserver joins the tunnel the moment it boots—before your build machine even finishes SSHing in.
1. Read the token your build machine saved:sh
TUNNEL_TOKEN="$(/bin/cat "${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat")"
Use code with caution.
2. Inject it into your cloud-init user_data template (or replace a placeholder like __CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN__ before calling ProvisionServer.sh):yaml
#cloud-config
runcmd:
  - mkdir -p /usr/share/keyrings
  - curl -fsSL https://cloudflare.com | tee /usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg >/dev/null
  - echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg] https://cloudflare.com $(. /etc/os-release && echo "$VERSION_CODENAME") main" | tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/cloudflared.list >/dev/null
  - apt-get update -qq && apt-get install -y -qq cloudflared
  - cloudflared service install __CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN__
Use code with caution.

Method 2: From Your Build Machine Over SSH (During Webserver Setup)

If you prefer to keep cloud-init minimal and have your build machine install cloudflared alongside Nginx/Apache:
1. Copy InstallCloudflared.sh (from the previous step) to the webserver or execute it remotely over SSH from your build machine:sh
TUNNEL_TOKEN="$(/bin/cat "${BUILD_HOME}/runtimedata/cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat")"

/usr/bin/ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no root@"${WEBSERVER_IP}" \
    "sh -s" "${TUNNEL_TOKEN}" < "${BUILD_HOME}/services/dns/cloudflare/InstallCloudflared.sh"
Use code with caution.
2. As soon as that command finishes, systemd starts cloudflared on the webserver and opens 4 persistent outbound connections to Cloudflare's edge.

Method 3: Adding a 2nd, 3rd, or Autoscaled Webserver

When your autoscaler spins up another webserver (for example, web-2 on Multipass or Linode):
• Run the exact same step with the exact same cloudflare_tunnel_token.dat.
• You do not create a second tunnel or touch DNS. Cloudflare automatically sees two active cloudflared connectors under the same Tunnel ID and immediately begins round-robin load-balancing visitor traffic across both webservers.
• When you destroy web-2 during scale-down, its connector drops off and Cloudflare seamlessly routes 100% of traffic back to web-1.

Quick Verification Command

To verify that a webserver successfully joined the tunnel, run this inside the webserver (or via multipass exec <server_name> -- ...):
sh
sudo systemctl status cloudflared
Use code with caution.
You should see active (running) along with log lines confirming Registered tunnel connection across four edge locations.
